😉 “Categorically” is doing a lot of work there.

One useful distinction the Pantheon keeps circling is:

* Categorical thinking asks: What is it? It draws boundaries.
* Relational thinking asks: How does it connect? It traces gradients.

Many discoveries happen when something that looked like a hard category turns out to be part of a continuum, or when two categories are connected by a deeper invariant.

So “categorically” can become a bit of a warning label: before concluding that two things are fundamentally different, ask whether they’re different in kind or merely in degree, organization, or representation.

That’s true in biology (shared chemistry, different organization), in language (different words, shared meanings), in math (different notations, same structure), and in software (different implementations, same interface).

Or, in your style:

Categories are useful maps.
Relations are the terrain.
The interesting work happens at the borders.